<launch>
  <!-- smart_vlm: the complete AI module for ONE question.

       This launch is a disposable unit. The challenge relaunches the system for every
       language command so nothing carries over between questions (README "Evaluation"),
       and eval_orchestrator does the same: it spawns this launch, waits for the answer,
       then SIGINTs the whole process group. Every node here therefore starts fresh and
       dies together — there is no in-process reset path anywhere, by design.

         ros2 launch smart_vlm smart_vlm.launch

       Startup ordering matters, and is enforced by latched gates rather than sleeps:

         sam_node boots UNARMED (wait_for_prompts) and loads weights   ~60 s
           -> smart_vlm sees sam + qwen ready      -> /pipeline/ready   (harness may ask)
           -> the reasoner extracts targets        -> /sam3/set_prompts
           -> sam_node arms, fresh session+run_dir -> /sam3/prompts_ack
           -> smart_vlm                            -> /pipeline/armed
                                                   -> /start_exploration (TARE drives)

       The reasoner is inside that chain, not downstream of it: if it never publishes
       /sam3/set_prompts the robot never moves and the question times out rather than
       answering wrongly.

       Nothing consumes the scene until /pipeline/armed, so the detector never sees a
       frame while it is still loading weights or still holding placeholder prompts —
       that is why TARE is held at /start_exploration, and why the bag in
       eval_bag.launch waits on /pipeline/armed.

       Inference backend is config/vqa.yaml (`vlm_backend` / `target_extract_backend`), not a
       launch argument. Nothing has to be started by hand first, because at eval
       there is nobody to start it: the organizers build the image and run this
       launch, and that is the whole procedure. `just vqa-up` remains only as a
       dev convenience for keeping a server resident ACROSS relaunches, which is
       a speed-up, not a requirement. -->

  <!-- Perception -->
  <arg name="sam_config" default="sam3_mecanum_sim.yaml"/> <!-- sam_mapper config file name -->

  <!-- Exploration. Which tare_planner config drives coverage.
       cmu_challenge is ours — a copy of the vendored indoor_small tuning that also
       holds the robot until the supervisor opens the gate (kAutoStart: false).
       indoor_small | indoor_large | outdoor are the upstream scenarios, and all
       three auto-start, so selecting one here means the robot drives before SAM
       is armed. -->
  <arg name="tare_scenario" default="cmu_challenge"/>

  <!-- Mission budget (README "Timing": 10 minutes per question, from startup) -->
  <arg name="question_budget_s"  default="600.0"/> <!-- hard limit, includes model load -->
  <arg name="explore_timeout_s"  default="300.0"/> <!-- exploration CAP; target coverage usually ends it sooner -->

  <!-- Exploration order. true: TARE sweeps the room on its own frontier exploration first and
       target viewpoints are withheld, so the tour is solved over the whole grid; the phase ends
       when TARE reports finished or the cap below elapses, and target-driven exploration then
       runs exactly as it always has. Discovery first, because a target the robot never drove
       past is one no amount of re-viewing can find. false restores the old behaviour, where
       targets compete with the frontier from the first second. -->
  <arg name="tare_explore_priority" default="true"/>
  <arg name="tare_explore_max_time" default="180.0"/>  <!-- cap; TARE finishing ends it sooner -->

  <arg name="answer_reserve_s"   default="90.0"/>  <!-- T-90: stop exploring no matter what -->
  <arg name="fallback_reserve_s" default="30.0"/>  <!-- T-30: publish a guess rather than nothing -->
  <arg name="fallback_count"     default="1"/>     <!-- the numerical guess of last resort -->
  <arg name="ready_timeout_s"    default="300.0"/> <!-- give up waiting on models, run degraded -->

  <!-- Category 3 only. An instruction question is answered by DRIVING, so the supervisor
       raises its own answering reserve to CATEGORY_ANSWER_RESERVE_S[3] the moment it
       classifies one (mission_clock.budget_for); these are the reasoner's half of the same
       arithmetic. route_reserve_s is when it must STOP driving, leaving room to settle on the
       goal pose and be torn down. Every waypoint is separately bounded by the try budget
       below, so no single leg can eat the journey. Demo routes run 1.7 to 33 m. -->
  <arg name="route_reserve_s"  default="45.0"/>
  <!-- Driving is three tries and two arrival tests. A try publishes the waypoint for
       try_duration_s; the leg ends when the robot is within settle_radius_m of the model's
       point OR within arrival_m of the point actually published, then settles for settle_s;
       otherwise the next try starts, and after max_tries the route moves on. Worst case per
       waypoint is max_tries * try_duration_s + settle_s = 50 s.
       settle_radius_m IS the scoring radius. arrival_m is the second test because the first
       cannot always be won: distance to the model's point is bounded below by the snap plus
       the converter's 0.3 m stop deadband, so a leg snapped 1.4 m off can never enter a 1.5 m
       circle and would burn all three tries standing exactly where it was aimed. -->
  <arg name="try_duration_s"   default="15.0"/>
  <arg name="max_tries"        default="3"/>
  <arg name="settle_radius_m"  default="1.5"/>
  <arg name="settle_s"         default="5.0"/>
  <arg name="arrival_m"        default="0.5"/>
  <!-- Before publishing, a waypoint is moved to the CLOSEST point of the traversable area, and
       re-chosen every metre of travel. The area is a grid built from /terrain_map_ext across
       the whole run, starting during exploration: that cloud decays in 4 s, so ground near a
       distant waypoint is not in any single frame of it and only accumulation makes the snap
       possible. Nothing is traded against closeness: snap_clearance_m erodes the area before
       the snap and is OFF, because the base autonomy applies its own 0.75 m when it decides
       where to drive, and reproducing that here only pushed our published point ~1.2 m off the
       waypoint. Dial it up to trade closeness for the converter passing our point through
       untouched. max_snap_m is the honesty limit: past it the nearest floor is not the edge of
       the object the model meant, so the waypoint is published unchanged. -->
  <arg name="snap_clearance_m"    default="0.1"/>
  <arg name="max_snap_m"          default="1.0"/>
  <!-- The floor grid. The cell is the quantisation floor on how precisely we can aim; the span
       only sets how often the grid has to grow, and 40 m square costs 0.16 MB.
       The cell matches terrain analysis' own scanVoxelSize; finer marks holes, not a region. -->
  <arg name="traversable_cell_m"      default="0.10"/>
  <arg name="traversable_half_span_m" default="20.0"/>
  <arg name="map_wait_s"       default="15.0"/> <!-- obj_map.json can land just after explore_done -->

  <!-- Reasoning -->
  <arg name="vqa_timeout_s" default="180.0"/>            <!-- max wait for a VLM reply -->

  <!-- Qwen server + /pipeline/ready gate: follows vqa.yaml `vlm_backend` /
       `target_extract_backend` (NEED_LOCAL_VQA). true when either call is local. -->
  <arg name="local_vqa"
       default="$(eval &quot;__import__('captioner.vlm_backends.constants', fromlist=['local_vqa_launch_flag']).local_vqa_launch_flag()&quot;)"/>
  <arg name="vqa_model"        default="qwen3vl"/>
  <arg name="vqa_quantization" default="int4"/>
  <!-- How many best-view ranks the reasoner answers from at once. Rank 1 alone misses
       objects it does not happen to frame; each extra view costs a full image of
       visual tokens. -->
  <arg name="max_context_views" default="3"/>
  <!-- Where this question's best-view crops are saved, under the crops root from the
       sam config. The eval harness passes "<sweep>/<scene>/<question id>-<question>";
       empty falls back to a run name with a random id in it, fine for a one-off. -->
  <arg name="run_id" default=""/>
  <!-- Save the crops and skip the answering call — how the best-view cache is built.
       Target extraction still runs, so SAM is armed exactly as on a scored run. -->
  <arg name="crops_only" default="false"/>
  <!-- How the object-reference reasoner chooses among the 3D map's candidates:
       vlm (the model chooses from the candidate table and marked views) |
       hybrid (geometry, with a model call only where the geometry is not decisive) |
       solver | naive. See smart_vlm/cat2_utils.py. -->
  <arg name="cat2_mode" default="vlm"/>

  <!-- 0. Qwen VQA server (local backend only) — before SAM, so the two model loads
          overlap instead of queueing. Deliberately absent on the cloud path: it would
          be 8.3 GB loaded per question for a model nothing queries.
          Do NOT also run `just vqa-up` when this is on — two servers would claim the
          same node name and the same /qwen_vqa topics.
       Output:  /qwen_vqa/{status,response} -->
  <include file="$(find-pkg-share captioner)/launch/vqa_server.launch" if="$(var local_vqa)">
    <arg name="model"        value="$(var vqa_model)"/>
    <arg name="quantization" value="$(var vqa_quantization)"/>
  </include>

  <!-- 1. SAM 3 detector — FIRST, so weights load while everything else comes up.
          Boots unarmed: this pipeline's prompts come from the question, so anything
          detected before /sam3/set_prompts would be against placeholder objects.
       Input:   /camera/image
       Output:  /sam3/{detections,instance_map,status,prompts_ack,best_view_dir} -->
  <include file="$(find-pkg-share sam_mapper)/launch/sam_node.launch">
    <arg name="config" value="$(var sam_config)"/>
    <arg name="wait_for_prompts" value="true"/>
  </include>

  <!-- 1b. 3D instance mapper — fuses SAM 3 masks with lidar into persistent 3D objects.
          Separate process from sam_node on purpose: SAM 3 inference and high-frequency ROS
          callbacks do not share a GIL well (20-80 s/frame when they did).
          Latest-frame-wins, so it drops under load rather than lagging the map.
       Input:   /sam3/{instance_map,detections}, /registered_scan, /state_estimation
       Output:  /obj_points, /obj_boxes, /obj_labels, /obj_map_json,
                /exploration/object_targets -->
  <include file="$(find-pkg-share sam_mapper)/launch/map_node.launch">
    <arg name="config" value="$(var sam_config)"/>
  </include>

  <!-- 2. Supervisor — mission clock, readiness/arming gates, guaranteed answer.
       Input:   /challenge_question, /sam3/{status,prompts_ack}, /qwen_vqa/status,
                /state_estimation, /numerical_response, /selected_object_marker
       Output:  /pipeline/{ready,armed,explore_done}, /smart_vlm/status,
                /numerical_response (T-30 fallback only) -->
  <node pkg="smart_vlm" exec="smart_vlm" name="smart_vlm_node" output="screen">
    <param name="question_budget_s"  value="$(var question_budget_s)"/>
    <param name="explore_timeout_s"  value="$(var explore_timeout_s)"/>
    <param name="answer_reserve_s"   value="$(var answer_reserve_s)"/>
    <param name="fallback_reserve_s" value="$(var fallback_reserve_s)"/>
    <param name="fallback_count"     value="$(var fallback_count)"/>
    <param name="ready_timeout_s"    value="$(var ready_timeout_s)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- 3. Numerical reasoner — question to targets to one count question asked of the
       top max_context_views best views together.
       Input:   /challenge_question, /gt_target_objects, /pipeline/explore_done,
                /sam3/best_view_dir, /qwen_vqa/response
       Output:  /sam3/set_prompts, /qwen_vqa/request, /numerical_response -->
  <node pkg="smart_vlm" exec="numerical_reasoner" name="numerical_reasoner" output="screen">
    <param name="run_id" value="$(var run_id)"/>
    <param name="crops_only" value="$(var crops_only)"/>
    <param name="vqa_timeout_s" value="$(var vqa_timeout_s)"/>
    <param name="max_context_views" value="$(var max_context_views)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- 3b. Object-reference reasoner — question to targets to one object of the 3D map,
       published as the answer Marker. Runs alongside the counting reasoner rather than
       instead of it: both classify the question first and only one of them ever acts, so
       one launch serves either category and nothing has to know which is coming.
       Input:   /challenge_question, /gt_target_objects, /pipeline/explore_done,
                /sam3/best_view_dir, /qwen_vqa/response
       Output:  /sam3/set_prompts, /qwen_vqa/request, /selected_object_marker -->
  <node pkg="smart_vlm" exec="object_reference_reasoner" name="object_reference_reasoner"
        output="screen">
    <param name="run_id" value="$(var run_id)"/>
    <param name="crops_only" value="$(var crops_only)"/>
    <param name="vqa_timeout_s" value="$(var vqa_timeout_s)"/>
    <param name="max_context_views" value="$(var max_context_views)"/>
    <param name="mode" value="$(var cat2_mode)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- 3c. Instruction reasoner — question to targets to an ordered Pose2D sequence.
       Arms SAM (so TARE can start), then after explore_done holds TARE, asks the VLM for
       a route over the 3D map plus the tagged best views, and drives it.
       Input:   /challenge_question, /gt_target_objects, /pipeline/explore_done,
                /sam3/best_view_dir, /state_estimation, /way_point_reached
       Output:  /sam3/set_prompts, /start_exploration (false), /way_point_with_heading -->
  <node pkg="smart_vlm" exec="instruction_reasoner" name="instruction_reasoner"
        output="screen">
    <param name="run_id" value="$(var run_id)"/>
    <param name="vqa_timeout_s" value="$(var vqa_timeout_s)"/>
    <!-- The reasoner keeps its own clock on the same budget as the supervisor's, so both
         agree on how much of the question is left without publishing it to each other. -->
    <param name="question_budget_s" value="$(var question_budget_s)"/>
    <param name="route_reserve_s"   value="$(var route_reserve_s)"/>
    <param name="try_duration_s"    value="$(var try_duration_s)"/>
    <param name="max_tries"         value="$(var max_tries)"/>
    <param name="settle_radius_m"   value="$(var settle_radius_m)"/>
    <param name="settle_s"          value="$(var settle_s)"/>
    <param name="arrival_m"         value="$(var arrival_m)"/>
    <param name="snap_clearance_m"    value="$(var snap_clearance_m)"/>
    <param name="max_snap_m"          value="$(var max_snap_m)"/>
    <param name="traversable_cell_m"      value="$(var traversable_cell_m)"/>
    <param name="traversable_half_span_m" value="$(var traversable_half_span_m)"/>
    <param name="map_wait_s"        value="$(var map_wait_s)"/>
    <param name="max_views"         value="$(var max_context_views)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- 3c. Target-driven exploration — which side of each target object has never been
       seen, turned into places to stand. Sits between perception and TARE and steers it
       without writing waypoints: TARE stays the single writer of /way_point_with_heading.
       Emits nothing until the reasoner publishes this question's targets, so a run with no
       targets extracted explores exactly as it did before.

       It also supplies half of the stop condition: /exploration/target_coverage_done goes
       true once every target label has an instance and every one of them is covered or
       proven blocked. The supervisor closes exploration only when TARE's own
       /exploration_finish has landed too — target coverage alone is unsound for a COUNT,
       since the objects we happened to find say nothing about the ones we did not. The
       explore window is the cap for scenes where neither ever comes.

       Every knob is a node parameter with its default in target_coverage.default_params,
       sourced from the mapper's own config where one exists (cluster thresholds, max range).
       Override with -p on the command line or add a <param> here.
       Input:   /sam3/{set_prompts,prompts_ack}, /exploration/object_targets,
                /exploration/target_viewpoint_feedback, /state_estimation,
                /pipeline/explore_done, /exploration_finish
       Output:  /exploration/{target_viewpoints,target_preempt,target_coverage_done,
                target_status}, <run_dir>/target_coverage.json -->
  <node pkg="smart_vlm" exec="target_explorer" name="target_explorer" output="screen">
    <!-- Same scenario the planner below is launched with, so target_explorer reads its
         kMaxTargetViewPointNum / kTargetViewPointSnapMaxDist out of the very file TARE is
         running on instead of keeping its own copy of them. -->
    <param name="tare_scenario" value="$(var tare_scenario)"/>
    <param name="tare_explore_priority" value="$(var tare_explore_priority)"/>
    <param name="tare_explore_max_time" value="$(var tare_explore_max_time)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- 4. TARE exploration — drives the robot to cover the scene.
       Held until the supervisor publishes /start_exploration, which it does alongside
       /pipeline/armed the instant SAM holds this question's prompts. The wait is
       kAutoStart: false in cmu_challenge.yaml, not a launch argument — see
       explore.launch for why an override cannot win against the scenario yaml.
       Without it the robot would spend the first ~60 s covering the scene against a
       detector still loading weights, ground that then never gets re-explored.
       Runs unconditionally, including under eval_bag.launch: keeping the node set
       identical to the scored configuration is worth more than the CPU it costs
       replaying a bag, where its waypoints simply have no consumer.
       Also steerable: target_explorer above publishes /exploration/target_viewpoints,
       which TARE snaps to real candidate viewpoints and treats as must-visit. Those are
       desiderata, not commands — anything not near a collision-free, connected candidate is
       dropped, and if the topic goes silent TARE reverts to stock coverage after
       kTargetViewPointTimeout. Still the only writer of /way_point_with_heading.
       Input:   /registered_scan, /terrain_map, /terrain_map_ext, /state_estimation,
                /start_exploration, /exploration/{target_viewpoints,target_preempt}
       Output:  /way_point_with_heading (Pose2D) -->
  <include file="$(find-pkg-share tare_planner)/launch/explore.launch">
    <arg name="scenario" value="$(var tare_scenario)"/>
  </include>

</launch>
